$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(-\dfrac{x}{y}\right)
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{x}{y}\right)
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}(x)\cdot y-x\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}}{y^2}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{(1)\cdot y-x\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}}{y^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{x\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}-y}{y^2}
\end{align}$

Now let's plug $\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\dfrac{x}{y}}$ into the expression we got:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}&=\dfrac{x\cdot\goldD{\dfrac{dy}{dx}}-y}{y^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{x\cdot\left(\goldD{-\dfrac{x}{y}}\right)-y}{y^2}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^3}
\end{align}$

hints.content:
Finally, let's plug $\blueD{x=4}$ and $\maroonD{y=3}$ into the expression we got:

$\begin{align}
\left.-\dfrac{\blueD x^2+\maroonD y^2}{\maroonD y^3}\right\rvert_{(\blueD{4},\maroonD{3})}&=-\dfrac{(\blueD{4})^2+(\maroonD{3})^2}{(\maroonD{3})^3}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{25}{27}
\end{align}$
